==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|haso}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*hasô}}, from an Indo-European root originally meaning [[grey]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhaːzə/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-Hase.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}
* {{rhymes|aːzə|lang=de}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|m|Hasen|Hasen|Häschen|dim2=Häslein|f=Häsin}}

# [[hare]] {{gloss|animal of either sex}}
# {{lb|de|constellation}} the [[constellation]] [[Lepus]]

====Usage notes====
* While English-speakers tend to mistakenly use the word “rabbit” for hares, the German tendency is reverse: ''Hase'' is sometimes mistakenly used instead of {{m|de|Kaninchen}}, and it tends to be the preferred word whenever the distinction is irrelevant or impossible to tell. (For example, a [[bunny girl]] is a {{m|de|Häschen}} in German and the [[Easter bunny]] is called {{m|de|Osterhase}}.)

